# Build the simplest thing that meets the requirement

<!-- core -->
**Complexity is opt-in.** Write the simplest implementation that satisfies what was actually asked
for. If something more elaborate is wanted, the user will say so — until then the elaborate version
is not the careful choice, it is the wrong one. Always on, every project and every subagent.

Derive state rather than storing it. No abstraction until a second caller exists. No configurability
nobody asked for. No speculative cases.

**Correctness, security and stated requirements always win** — when the simple version genuinely
won't do, say why in one sentence and build the one that works. If what you are building outgrows the
requirement mid-task, cut it back then rather than refactoring later.

<!-- /core -->

The test is the requirement, not a judgment about what might be needed later. A design handling cases
nobody asked for isn't more robust — it's more code to read, more state to keep consistent, and more
surface for a defect to hide in.

## The specific habits

- **Derive state; don't store it.** A value computable from what you already have needs no field, no
  `remember`, no state holder, no synchronisation rule. Storing it creates a second source of truth
  and the obligation to keep both agreeing.
- **No abstraction until there is a second caller.** One implementation needs no interface, strategy,
  registry or factory. Add the seam when the second case arrives and shows you where it belongs.
- **No configurability nobody asked for.** A hard-coded value that is right beats an option never set
  to anything else.
- **No speculative cases.** Don't handle inputs, states or failure modes the requirement doesn't
  mention and the code can't produce.
- **Fewer moving parts beats fewer lines.** Simple means less to reason about, not tersest syntax. A
  clear loop beats a clever one-liner.

## The limits

**Correctness, security and stated requirements always win.** This governs the space where several
correct designs exist and one is smaller — it is never licence to under-build or ignore a real
constraint.

## The incident behind it

Gymie KAN-144 needed one behaviour: after logging a set, focus the next set's weight field. It
shipped as a dedicated focus-state holder with its own tracking, update rules and synchronisation
against workout state. It was later replaced by deriving the focused input from the current set —
removing 67 lines of `commonMain` and a whole class of desynchronisation bug.

The derived version was always available, met the requirement exactly, and should have been written
first. The user's own statement of the requirement:

> *"focus should be easy no? If already started and is a log then focus its weight field. Otherwise
> just go next after user action."*
